WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.935
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Tom Holland.

00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:05.304
I want to talk to you.
I'm very impressed by the fact

00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:06.706
that even with
your busy schedule,

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.474
you find time
for charitable work.

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:10.310
-Yes.
-Especially with your family

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:11.477
helping out.

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:14.180
Talk about
the Brothers Trust with us.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.948
-The Brothers Trust.
Wow. I think --

00:00:15.948 --> 00:00:18.184
The Brothers Trust is, I think,

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.152
my greatest accomplishment
in my career.

00:00:20.152 --> 00:00:21.654
It's something
I'm incredibly proud of.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.623
Something that also wouldn't
necessarily have happened

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.524
without my lovely mum and dad.

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:27.927
My mum really runs the charity.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.528
You know, she's the driving
force for the whole thing,

00:00:29.528 --> 00:00:32.065
but my brothers and I
are the ones that, you know,

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:35.601
we take up the fundraising side
of things, which is really fun,

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:37.570
and we do it socially
and all that sort of stuff.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.207
And the Brothers Trust
is essentially a vehicle

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.642
to shed light on charities
that don't necessarily

00:00:43.642 --> 00:00:45.478
get the exposure
that they deserve.

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:48.113
You know, there are charities
out there that are famous,

00:00:48.113 --> 00:00:51.383
and we think that
that's kind of wrong, you know?

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:54.053
We want these smaller charities
that really make a difference,

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:56.422
day in, day out, to be helped.

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:57.690
So that's kind of
what we're doing.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.258
-And it's nice.
I think a lot of those smaller,

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:02.928
more grassroots charities are
more connected to communities

00:01:02.928 --> 00:01:04.864
that need their help,
and it's just wonderful

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:06.866
that you shine a light on that.
-Thank you.

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:09.401
And I mean, for us, we've got
a charity called Alex's Wish,

00:01:09.401 --> 00:01:12.038
which is about a mum
searching for a cure

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:14.807
for her son's disease, and
because we know her personally,

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:16.909
we feel much more invested
in the cause,

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:19.479
and it gives us
that extra bit of drive

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:22.214
to go the extra way
and raise that extra dollar

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:23.249
to make a difference.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.518
So it's been
incredibly rewarding.

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:28.621
And, also, to see the difference
that it's made

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:32.458
and the smiles on kids' faces
is the best thing.

00:01:32.458 --> 00:01:34.494
-And it's really cool,
especially when you talk about

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:37.163
the smiles on kids' faces.
Like, you know,

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:38.964
one of the nicest things
about the position you're in

00:01:38.964 --> 00:01:40.266
is, obviously,
you're playing something

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.502
that means a lot to kids.
-Yeah, of course.

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.271
-The famous Spider-Man quote,
when you have a lot of power,

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.505
responsibility is also something

00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:47.907
you have to take
equally serious.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.042
-Absolutely. Absolutely. When --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:52.412
No, wait, hang on a minute.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:54.547
"With --"
What is it again?

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:55.882
-I think the way I said it
is wrong.

00:01:55.882 --> 00:02:01.187
-I think you were right.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:03.455
-Last time you were here,
we talked about how you were

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:05.892
taking up golf,
and we remarked upon it

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:09.462
that I would imagine, like,
again, you're precious cargo.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:12.064
Nobody --
Much like catching COVID,

00:02:12.064 --> 00:02:14.100
which shut everything down,
if you got an injury,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.202
that would be bad, as well,

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:17.903
for the good people
that run the studio.

00:02:17.903 --> 00:02:19.639
Golf, they must have been happy.
They must have been like,

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:22.208
"Okay. I think he's gonna
be safe, injury-wise."

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:25.478
-Yeah, I don't think
they realize how much drinking

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:27.480
is involved with golf.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.416
-Right, right, right.
-I go and play golf,

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:32.318
and they're like,
"What happened to him yesterday?

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:34.620
He can't remember
any of his lines."

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.155
That's not true, guys.
I'm very professional.

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:38.357
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-But I love golf.

00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:39.725
Golf is a great way
to switch off.

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:41.160
It's a great way
to stay competitive

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.661
while also staying safe.
-Yeah.

00:02:42.661 --> 00:02:44.931
-You know, the day we finished
"Spider-Man 3,"

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:48.901
my brothers and I
flew to Montana and went skiing.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:52.405
We obviously didn't tell Sony,
and then they found out,

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.106
and they were like,
"Where are you?"

00:02:54.106 --> 00:02:56.743
Like, the top of a black run,
like, [bleep] myself.

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.378
[ Laughter ]
But, yeah, no.

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:00.113
Golf, for me,
has been a big thing.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:02.047
We travel the world,
we play a lot of golf,

00:03:02.047 --> 00:03:03.416
and it's a lot of fun.

00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:05.117
-You got to go to the Masters.
-Yes.

00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:08.421
-Did you -- I mean --
I would imagine the worst part

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:09.655
is that, at a time like that,
where you want

00:03:09.655 --> 00:03:11.924
to watch something
and be a little anonymous,

00:03:11.924 --> 00:03:14.093
it's obviously hard for you.
But was it cool?

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:15.728
Was the experience of going
to the Masters great?

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:18.931
-I mean, if there's ever
a silver lining for COVID,

00:03:18.931 --> 00:03:20.933
you know, we were able
to be there at a time

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:24.337
where they would only let 3,000
people onto the course.

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:25.704
Normally, it's 60,000 people,

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:28.074
so for me,
it was the perfect time to go.

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:29.642
I kept bumping into celebrities.

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:32.011
Like, "Dude, how crazy is this?"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:34.179
You know?
And it was a lot of fun.

00:03:34.179 --> 00:03:36.816
I remember being on the 10th tee
watching Tony Finau tee off,

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:40.320
and, for me, I'm an actor,
I live in this world,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.954
I meet actors all the time,
so it's something

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:44.757
I'm not that impressed by
when I meet other actors.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:48.494
But if I meet athletes,
mate, it blows me away.

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:51.430
And we're on the 10th tee
watching Tony Finau tee off,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.934
and Ian Poulter just rocks up,
gin and tonic in hand,

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.835
and, honestly,
it was the highlight of my year.

00:03:56.835 --> 00:03:58.204
It was amazing.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:00.306
Just getting
all the juicy golf goss.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:04.643
-Oh. If you can meet an athlete
who's willing to tell you

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:07.413
the stories, it is
the greatest thing in the world.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.749
-He definitely had a few gin
and tonics, I'll tell you that.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:11.784
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.719
-I mean, I've always found
there's a bit of a trade-off.

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:16.856
Like, when I meet an athlete and
they give me a really juicy one,

00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:19.592
then I have to give them --
I feel like they're like,

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:21.760
"And then could you tell me
something about show business?"

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.095
-Right, right, right.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:23.095 --> 00:04:25.465
And you're like, "Yeah,
everyone's really horrible."

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:29.135
-Yeah. I'm like, "So, uh,
Tom does this lube thing."

00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:32.905
-Yeah, right!
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.673
-"Don't tell anybody!
He only did it twice!

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:37.176
He only did it twice!"

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:39.746
Hey, man, we were just about
to get to the place

00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:41.047
where you were gonna
tell me all the spoilers,

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:42.548
but we've run out of time.
-Sure.

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:43.916
I can tell you.
-No, no, no.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.051
We're out of time.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:46.051 --> 00:04:48.921
We're out of time!
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.723
-I'll tweet it later.
Don't worry.

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:52.157
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:52.157 --> 00:04:54.961
-You guys, that's Tom Holland.
-Thank you so much.

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:57.429
-"Spider-Man: No Way Home"
exclusively in theaters and IMAX

00:04:57.429 --> 00:04:58.765
on Friday, December 17th.

